  <h2>Decide what you need to prove before ordering another sample</h2>
  <p>For a new leggings pattern, start by checking waistband position, coverage and movement in the base size. Once the fit is agreed, choose size-set samples that test your actual grading risk. For example, an order covering several size ranges may warrant checks at the ends of the run and where a pattern block changes. Record which sizes were tried so the approval is meaningful later.</p>
  <p>For an existing stock style with a new logo, ask to see the right garment and the proposed application. Document which colour and size were reviewed, the logo location and the artwork revision. If the colour or material changes, decide what needs fresh approval. Agree sample scope and any fees with the supplier before work begins.</p>

  <h2>Approve custom colour on the intended material</h2>
  <p>For a stock colour, ask for the current colour reference and confirm availability. For a custom shade, send a physical reference or an agreed colour standard and ask whether a lab dip is appropriate. Record the approved reference, fabric and viewing conditions. For a matching top and bottom in different fabrics, compare the two together. Similar colour names can still produce an obvious mismatch on a finished set.</p>
  <p>Check bulk shade against the agreed reference if colour is critical. For dye transfer, fading or contrast trim staining, use separate <a href="/blogs/activewear-colorfastness-testing-guide/">colourfastness checks</a>.</p>

  <h2>Put the approval rule in the order brief</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Use one revision for the tech pack, artwork, measurement sheet and approved sample.</li>
    <li>List any exceptions on the PP sample, including substitute fabric, missing labels or unapproved colour. Assign an owner and date to close each one.</li>
    <li>Agree which approvals are required before bulk cutting, which checks happen during production, and who signs off if the results differ.</li>
    <li>If a TOP sample is required, identify the production lot and define how the supplier should handle deviations before the rest of the order moves forward.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Retail vendor manuals illustrate how these gates can differ by buyer. <a href="https://www.dxl.com/media/content/vendors/Chapter6-ProductDevelopmentProcess-Privatelabel81822.pdf">DXL’s vendor guide</a> describes PP using intended bulk materials and trims. <a href="https://assets.ctfassets.net/6ufjjlh0gzjl/3kc9Ft8MiJ6wQRx0snFT1x/8b8dda6e946f0e8c77355cdca96c79ad/URBN_New_Vendor_Quick_Start_Guide_FAQ_-_10.15.24.pdf">URBN’s vendor guide</a> specifies pulling TOP samples from actual bulk production. Agree the requirements for your own order.</p>
